# F206 split the one `push`/`insert`/`set` arm three ways so each name's message could key on the
# type it was given rather than on the name; F207 then deleted two of the three, `push` and
# `insert` having come out with one message each. So most of this fixture now comes from the
# *resolver*, and the lines still differ by type -- which is the thing a .err substring cannot
# assert and the reason this is a .diag.
#
# **The last three lines changed on 2026-10-26, when `set`'s arm went too and the fixture caught
# what that cost.** Each used to be keyed on the type: a set was told about `add`, a MutableArray
# about `b[i] = value`. `#[when_unmatched]` is one line for a whole set, so both halves are merged
# into one and every unmatched `set` now gets both -- F190's criterion, the content survives and
# the aim does not. The first two also needed a fix to reach the hint at all: `set(aSet, item)` has
# two arguments, which match only the `Mutable<T>` cell, so the call never reaches the resolver and
# read "expects Mutable<int>, found Set<int>" and nothing else -- true, and useless, since nothing
# about a set suggests a cell. `when_unmatched_for_name` makes the single-candidate path read the
# hint set-wide, which is how the resolver had always read it.
#
# The two `insert` arity lines are the F207 fix in place: the set's `#[when_unmatched]` advice used
# to be appended only to the per-argument messages, so `insert(aSet, x)` -- where both declarations
# are eliminated on arity before any type is compared -- said "no declaration of 'insert' takes 2
# argument(s)" and nothing else. The advice is about the name, not about which check ran last.
type 31:14 'push' expects [T] or MutableArray<T>, found Map<string, int> -- the other collections have their own word
type 32:14 'push' expects [T] or MutableArray<T>, found Set<int> -- the other collections have their own word
type 35:14 no declaration of 'insert' takes 2 argument(s) -- 'insert' places a value at an index and has no collection form; use set(map, key, value), add(set, item) or append(sb, s)
type 36:5 no declaration of 'insert' takes 2 argument(s) -- 'insert' places a value at an index and has no collection form
type 37:5 'insert' expects [T] or MutableArray<T>, found MutableMap<string, int> -- 'insert' places a value at an index
type 41:18 argument 1 of 'set' expects Mutable<int>, found Set<int> -- a set has no 'set' (use add(set, item) to add an item), and a MutableArray element is written with 'b[i] = value' rather than with 'set', which copies an array where a MutableArray is not copied
type 42:9 argument 1 of 'set' expects Mutable<int>, found MutableSet<int> -- a set has no 'set' (use add(set, item) to add an item), and a MutableArray element is written with 'b[i] = value' rather than with 'set', which copies an array where a MutableArray is not copied
type 43:5 'set' expects [T], Map<K, V>, Mutable<T> or a builder, found MutableArray<int> -- a set has no 'set' (use add(set, item) to add an item), and a MutableArray element is written with 'b[i] = value' rather than with 'set', which copies an array where a MutableArray is not copied
